  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nCurrently, slow moving mid level low over the Upper Mississippi\nValley continues to push east, with weak upper level ridging\nover the Rockies and south central Canada. Lingering boundary\nlayer moisture continues to result in diurnally driven CU over\nsome areas of the local region, which has been on the decrease\nthe past few hours. Comfortable temperatures in the 60s this\nafternoon with light winds. \n\nWill see increasing southerly return flow for tomorrow as a lee\nside sfc low develops across the Northern High Plains in\nresponse to a decent mid level S/WV moving northeast out of the\nDesert Southwest into the Wyoming Rockies. The increased \npressure gradient will see breezy south/southeasterly winds \nduring the day Tuesday, though lack of WAA results in similar \ntemperatures as today (60's). \n\nThis wave continues northeast and moves over the Northern Plains\nfrom late Tuesday night before exiting Wednesday night. This\nfeature brings the best chances for precipitation this week, and\nalso may result in a few thunderstorms. \n\nS/WV ridging on Thursday brings the better WAA into the region\nfor the week, with forecast high temperatures closer to normal\nif not above, well into the 70s to mid 80s. Thereafter, a\nquasi-southwesterly flow develops supporting near normal highs\nand lows, and mainly dry weather. NBM and ensembles then suggest\nanother increase in POPs later next weekend/early next week,\nwhen the base of a western CONUS trough is projected to move\nthrough.",
